1
00:00:00,656 --> 00:00:03,685
I did already mention the term harness.

2
00:00:04,582 --> 00:00:05,191
What's that about?

3
00:00:05,784 --> 00:00:08,941
Well, in the end, tools like Claude Cowork,

4
00:00:09,085 --> 00:00:10,816
Microsoft Copilot, and so on,

5
00:00:11,569 --> 00:00:14,966
they offer agent harnesses. Or you could say

6
00:00:15,174 --> 00:00:17,738
they are agent harnesses. That's a

7
00:00:17,818 --> 00:00:19,949
definition question, but the core idea is

8
00:00:19,981 --> 00:00:20,526
the same.

9
00:00:21,263 --> 00:00:23,875
The app contains the harness, and in that

10
00:00:23,907 --> 00:00:27,384
harness runs a so-called agent loop. And all

11
00:00:27,657 --> 00:00:30,461
these things together then give us an AI

12
00:00:30,621 --> 00:00:31,439
agent, you could say.

13
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:34,115
To understand it, we have to take a step

14
00:00:34,243 --> 00:00:36,807
back and we have to take a look at what a

15
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:39,579
large language model actually is, because

16
00:00:39,691 --> 00:00:42,015
that is important. Because such a large

17
00:00:42,015 --> 00:00:44,979
language model is the brain of an agent. So

18
00:00:44,979 --> 00:00:47,816
we must understand what a large language

19
00:00:47,928 --> 00:00:50,299
model does and even more importantly, what

20
00:00:50,331 --> 00:00:51,245
it can't do.

21
00:00:51,774 --> 00:00:52,831
A large language model,

22
00:00:53,394 --> 00:00:55,632
is simply a next word guesser.

23
00:00:56,759 --> 00:00:59,463
Or to be precise, it outputs tokens, but

24
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:02,700
tokens are just partial words. We can simply

25
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,932
stick to word. That's easier to understand.

26
00:01:05,336 --> 00:01:07,972
So the whole idea behind a large language

27
00:01:07,972 --> 00:01:10,657
model is that you give it some input, some

28
00:01:10,657 --> 00:01:13,274
prompt, for example, a question or maybe an

29
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:15,806
incomplete sentence like the capital of

30
00:01:15,806 --> 00:01:16,677
France is...

31
00:01:18,564 --> 00:01:20,415
and then the model predicts

32
00:01:21,088 --> 00:01:24,453
what the next word could or should be. So

33
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:27,337
that may be Paris. It may also come to the

34
00:01:27,337 --> 00:01:29,357
conclusion, because there is randomness

35
00:01:29,357 --> 00:01:32,658
involved, that the or home should be the

36
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:35,094
next word because it goes for a slightly

37
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:38,010
different phrased sentence. Now, the next

38
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:40,286
word it predicts is entirely based on

39
00:01:40,398 --> 00:01:41,263
probabilities,

40
00:01:41,856 --> 00:01:44,725
probabilities that stem from the model

41
00:01:44,725 --> 00:01:47,577
training. So based on all the training data

42
00:01:48,010 --> 00:01:48,443
it saw,

43
00:01:48,987 --> 00:01:52,032
so to say, during its training run. Based on

44
00:01:52,032 --> 00:01:54,948
all that data, each next word has a certain

45
00:01:55,061 --> 00:01:57,432
probability attached to it. And therefore,

46
00:01:58,025 --> 00:01:58,730
in most cases,

47
00:01:59,307 --> 00:02:02,207
it may output Paris as the next word for

48
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:02,928
this example.

49
00:02:03,473 --> 00:02:05,572
In some cases, it may also output a

50
00:02:05,636 --> 00:02:08,024
different word though. It's a probability

51
00:02:08,056 --> 00:02:11,164
thing. And models work like this since

52
00:02:11,164 --> 00:02:14,001
ChatGPT was released. That has not changed.

53
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:15,539
It's still the case today.

54
00:02:16,613 --> 00:02:19,675
They take text as input. They output text.

55
00:02:20,237 --> 00:02:22,596
Now, this has an important implication

56
00:02:22,628 --> 00:02:22,788
though.

57
00:02:23,366 --> 00:02:24,104
By default,

58
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:28,390
a large language model can only write text.

59
00:02:29,064 --> 00:02:31,375
So how do we turn that into an agent that

60
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:34,007
can suddenly also read or write files?

61
00:02:34,841 --> 00:02:36,334
Well, we give it tools.

62
00:02:37,088 --> 00:02:39,014
If we give it a read file and a search the

63
00:02:39,078 --> 00:02:42,128
web tool, it can suddenly start reading and

64
00:02:42,128 --> 00:02:44,583
researching. If we give it write tools or

65
00:02:44,712 --> 00:02:47,167
even a tool to generate and run its own

66
00:02:47,199 --> 00:02:49,895
code, It can build its own little programs

67
00:02:50,007 --> 00:02:53,120
and essentially do anything on a machine. So

68
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:53,874
that's pretty good.

69
00:02:54,484 --> 00:02:55,591
The only problem is

70
00:02:56,169 --> 00:02:59,057
the way a large language model works hasn't

71
00:02:59,057 --> 00:03:02,475
changed. It can't read a file. It can't

72
00:03:02,507 --> 00:03:05,636
perform a web search. It can write code, but

73
00:03:05,636 --> 00:03:08,460
it can't run that code, neither on your

74
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:10,995
machine nor on the server where the model is

75
00:03:11,092 --> 00:03:11,316
running.

76
00:03:11,910 --> 00:03:13,787
Because that's also important as a side

77
00:03:13,819 --> 00:03:15,584
note, the model is not running on your

78
00:03:15,584 --> 00:03:16,900
machine. It's running somewhere in the

79
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:17,253
cloud.

80
00:03:17,847 --> 00:03:21,096
But either way, it can't use tools. So how

81
00:03:21,226 --> 00:03:23,393
do we then give it tools and give it more

82
00:03:23,523 --> 00:03:24,305
capabilities?
